(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the measurement principles of Digital Storage Oscilloscopes \(DSOs\): Acquisition memory depth, trigger modes, and aliasing.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Digital Storage Oscilloscope \(DSO\) Operation:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Acquisition Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   Input signals pass through calibrated analog attenuators and wideband preamplifiers before) Tj
T*
(   conversion by high-speed flash ADCs \(typically 1 to 5 GSample/sec\). Digital samples are) Tj
T*
(   continuously clocked into high-speed Acquisition Memory buffers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Memory Depth Relationship:) Tj
T*
(   $$\\text{Sampling Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Memory Depth}}{\\text{Time Window}}$$) Tj
T*
(   A deep memory buffer allows maintaining high real-time sampling rates even at slow timebase) Tj
T*
(   settings, preventing waveform aliasing and permitting zoomed inspection of high-frequency) Tj
T*
(   switching glitches.) Tj
T*
ET

